Côte d'Ivoire,Digital Economy,Why Did the Marshall Islands Issue Its Sovereign Debt as USDM1?,https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirAFBVV95cUxQSzhGNXhVOFBpWDRBMnhwQkFkZ0xuamV4TWkwRWlJMFRiRmZYQmFpdmpVZ0o4dWlSRE1HeU1CcWg3MWhMOHBqenFxS0pCdXpyd1dVOVMydFotdkVLbXJQSFgwaEcweHZ4ZU00S29MQ1lobWJWbzU4RUl1aW1ZTXpRSkRRNFU4eWw2TUNodjkyUGNCUlpIR0ZqUkQ4d1dGcnNfVXpUdzl4cWVPOVd3?oc=5,Disruption Banking,21 Oct 2026,,no,,,,no,yes,4,4
Côte d'Ivoire,Governance,PP110: Negotiating the state: Political participation in post-conflict Côte d’Ivoire,https://www.afrobarometer.org/publication/pp110-negotiating-the-state-political-participation-in-post-conflict-cote-divoire/,Afrobarometer,22 Oct 2026,,no,"Côte d’Ivoire has experienced recurring political instability since the death of President Félix  Houphouët-Boigny in 1993. The political battles that followed exposed longstanding tensions over citizenship and Ivoirité , deepening regional, ethnic, and political divisions. These tensions resulted in a 1999 military coup, election-related violence in 2000, a 2002 rebellion that divided the country, and the disputed 2010 presidential election, which triggered a violent post-election conflict that claimed more than 3,000 lives (Akindès, 2004; Cheeseman, 2015;  Wells & Dufka, 2011).","Political participation provides an important lens through which to understand democratic consolidation in post-conflict societies. It includes institutionalised forms of engagement, such as contacting elected representatives, as well as non-institutionalised activities such as protest (Theocharis & van Deth, 2017; van Deth, 2021). In fragile political environments,  participation is often shaped by institutional trust, citizens’ experiences of economic hardship,  cognitive engagement such as access to information and political discussion among friends and family, and perceptions of government responsiveness (Bratton & van de Walle, 1997;  Guseva, 2024; Verba, Schlozman, & Brady, 1995). In many African contexts, citizens may contact political leaders not only to seek assistance, but also to demand recognition and inclusion in political decision making. Protest, meanwhile, can provide a channel through which citizens express grievances and demand accountability (Klaus, Paller, & Wilfahrt, 2023;  Muller & Seligson, 1987; Harris & Hern, 2019).","Much of the political-science literature on Côte d’Ivoire has focused on citizenship, armed conflict, electoral violence, civil-military relations, and ethnic politics (Akindès, 2004; Schiel,  Faulkner, & Powell, 2017). While these studies have deepened understanding of the country’s political trajectory, less attention has been paid to how ordinary citizens engage with governance institutions in the post-conflict period. This paper addresses that gap by examining factors associated with two forms of political participation: contacting elected representatives and participating in demonstrations or protest marches.",yes,no,0,10
