Ghana,Agrifood & Climate,Scaling up Integrated Flood and Drought Management and Early Warning System for Climate Change Adaptation in the Volta Basin Countries (VFDM-Phase 2),https://wmo.int/media/project-update/from-policy-practice-burkina-faso-strengthens-early-warning-systems-and-climate-resilience,World Meteorological Organization WMO,23 Oct 2026,,no,"Burkina Faso marked the 76th World Meteorological Day with a series of high-level events highlighting the country’s growing capacity to anticipate and respond to climate-related risks, and the increasing integration of meteorological services into national development and resilience planning.","The official ceremony, held on 23 March and chaired by the Minister of State, Minister of Territorial Administration and Mobility, Mr Emile Zerbo, brought together government authorities, technical and financial partners, and members of the scientific community. Celebrated annually, World Meteorological Day commemorates the entry into force of the Convention of the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) and serves as a platform to highlight the essential role of meteorology in protecting lives and supporting sustainable development.","Under the 2026 theme, “ Observing today, protecting tomorrow ,” the event underscored the importance of strengthening observation systems as the foundation for effective early warning and climate services.",yes,no,0,10
Ghana,Agrifood & Climate,Heavy rains during critical period of cacao cultivation hit Ghana’s yields,https://news.mongabay.com/2026/10/heavy-rains-during-critical-period-of-cacao-cultivation-hit-ghanas-yields/,news - Mongabay,21 Oct 2026,,no,"Heavy rainfall during the cacao flowering season is a major driver of Ghana’s recent harvest fluctuations, new research finds.","A study published in September by researchers at the Harvard University Center for the Environment in the U.S. found that heavy rainfall during cacao’s flowering and early pod development is linked to significant yield decreases. The researchers described how short, untypical rains during the flowering season, together with dry-season drought, is a major reason for recent fluctuations in Ghana’s cacao production.","Ghana and Côte d’Ivoire together account for about 60% of global cocoa production, making West Africa central to the world’s chocolate supply. Cacao seeds are the primary ingredient in chocolate.",yes,no,1,9
Ghana,Agrifood & Climate,Martin County company welcomes Ghana representatives,https://www.fairmontsentinel.com/news/local-news/2026/10/24/martin-county-company-welcomes-ghana-representatives/,Fairmont Sentinel,24 Oct 2026,,no,"WELCOME - Four representatives from Ghana, including Deputy Minister of Food and Agriculture Hon. John Dumelo, visited with Easy Environmental Solutions in Welcome on Friday to discuss their EasyFEN fertilizer machine.","He was joined by Savanna Pride Ltd. CEO Issifu Harruna, Director Saaka Ahmed Dan and Team Member Nurudeen Shakibu. Easy Environmental Solutions Vice President of Global Operations Africa, Middle East and Europe, Nathan Carpenter, said with EasyFEN, they are able to help countries around the world create greater sovereignty from a fertilizer perspective.","""We’re looking at improving food security through localized production of readily available fertilizer through readily available resources,"" he said. ""Doing that on a commercial scale. Soil health is incredibly important to us, as well as it being affordable to the farmer. I can’t stress that enough. You can do really good things in this world, but if they end up costing the farmer more, you’re going to have that much more of a problem selling it. You need to have something affordable on the ground.""",yes,no,2,8
Ghana,Agrifood & Climate,The farmer growing a future under Ghana’s trees,https://www.positive.news/society/the-farmer-growing-a-future-under-ghanas-trees/,Positive News,22 Oct 2026,,no,"While some farmers are sceptical of the benefits of shade-growing, cocoa in its natural state is a forest plant, and grows best under a canopy. Susana’s cocoa plants do much better in the shade, she tells me: “They thrive when they’re not exposed to the sun”. So she’s adopted ‘agroforestry’ techniques, planting native trees such as African mahogany in between her crops. Cocoa farmers may be its beneficiaries, but it’s not just cocoa that the programme covers. It helps growers diversify into other ‘forest-friendly’ activities, including beekeeping, raising pigs and snails, and growing a wider range of vegetables. Susana ticks these off on her fingers – “peppers, pawpaw, plantain, cassava”.","When I ask her to list the benefits of being part of the programme, it’s clear that it covers an impressively wide scope, from the practical and down-to-earth – providing everything from cutlasses and sprayers to boots and knapsacks – to business advice on how to make the best use of improved profit margins. And that improvement can be quite substantial. I ask Susana how much. She pauses, calculating, and then: “About 80%!” she says with a grin. With advice from the programme, she’s set up a daycare nursery and a bakery, employing 17 workers.",,yes,no,4,6
