Mozambique,Agrifood & Climate,India–Mozambique rice trade and price outlook — CMB News,https://commodity-board.com/india-mozambique-rice-axis-logistics-prices-and-new-trade-potential,CMB News,26 Oct 2026,,no,"Indian FOB quotations from New Delhi have firmed steadily since late September. Non‑basmati steam types show clear week‑on‑week gains: Sharbati ""all steam"" rose from EUR 0.44/kg (FOB, 26 September) to EUR 0.50/kg by 24 October, PR11 ""all steam"" from EUR 0.31/kg to EUR 0.37/kg, and 1509 steam from EUR 0.64/kg to EUR 0.68/kg over the same period. Higher‑value parboiled 1121 steam increased from EUR 0.69/kg to EUR 0.75/kg, while 1121 creamy white sella advanced from EUR 0.59/kg to EUR 0.65/kg, indicating broad strength across the Indian long‑grain complex.","Premium and organic segments are comparatively stable at elevated levels. Organic white basmati from India remains at EUR 1.57/kg (FOB, New Delhi) and organic white non‑basmati at EUR 1.27/kg, unchanged since mid‑October. In Vietnam, FOB Hanoi prices are mostly sideways: long white 5% at EUR 0.35/kg, Jasmine at EUR 0.37/kg, Japonica and white glutinous at EUR 0.46/kg, Homali at EUR 0.51/kg, with specialty types such as red rice (EUR 0.64/kg), black rice (EUR 0.88/kg) and paper‑dried rice (EUR 1.68/kg) holding firm. This configuration points to mildly bullish Indian export values against a stable Vietnamese reference curve.","India and Mozambique are exploring tighter cooperation across agriculture and food processing, with rice singled out as a core commodity in bilateral trade. Mozambique currently imports around 600,000 tonnes of rice per year, and the country’s authorities emphasize that India already plays a central role in covering this demand. Recent discussions highlight Mozambique’s intention to attract Indian investment into primary production, modern milling and value‑added processing, creating more stable long‑term supply relationships for Indian exporters while supporting Mozambique’s food security.",yes,no,0,10
