Nigeria,Macroeconomy,"Why Oil Production, Asset Protection Matter to Nigeria’s 7% Economic Growth Target",https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/26/why-oil-production-asset-protection-matter-to-nigerias-7-economic-growth-target/,THISDAYLIVE,26 Oct 2026,,no,"Nigeria’s ambition to achieve seven per cent economic growth in 2026 will depend partly on its ability to increase crude oil production and protect the assets that sustain the industry. With crude oil exports accounting for a significant share of the country’s foreign exchange earnings, production losses arising from oil theft, pipeline vandalism and other disruptions could undermine efforts to boost revenue and expand the economy. As the government pursues its growth target amid domestic and external economic pressures, sustained oil production, effective asset protection and the role of Tantita Security Services Nigeria Limited in safeguarding oil infrastructure in the Niger Delta have become important considerations in the country’s economic outlook. Victoria Ojiako writes","Nigeria’s ambitious seven per cent Gross Domestic Product (GDP) growth forecast for this year will not be a tea party. The World Bank projects growth at 4.4 per cent, citing stronger resilience and better-than-expected economic performance.","The International Monetary Fund (IMF) forecasts real GDP growth at 4.1 per cent;PwC Nigeria estimates economic expansion at 4.2 per cent, driven by improved crude oil production and key sector performance.",yes,no,3,7
