Tanzania,Macroeconomy,"Mining and Agriculture Earn the Dollars, Oil Spends Them: What Tanzania's Dollar Gap Means for Growth, Day by Day",https://ticgl.com/mining-and-agriculture-earn-the-dollars-oil-spends-them-what-tanzanias-dollar-gap-means-for-growth-day-by-day/,Tanzania Investment and Consultant Group Ltd,20 Oct 2026,,no,"Prepared by TERI for research and strategic-information purposes only. It is not investment, legal, tax or financial advice and does not state the position of the Bank of Tanzania or the Government. All sector figures come from the Bank of Tanzania Domestic Financial Markets Weekly Report for 12-16 October 2026; figures marked ""TICGL calc."" are our arithmetic on those numbers. The report does not define ""purchases"" and ""sales"" in its sector table: we read purchases as dollars banks bought from customers (supply of dollars) and sales as dollars banks sold to customers (use of dollars). Price, import, external-balance and growth evidence in Tab 4 comes from the Bank of Tanzania Monthly Economic Review, September 2026 (data to August 2026). The link from weekly dollar flows to those outcomes is analytical, not a measured causal effect. One week of flow data is a short window: patterns may not persist.","Tanzania's dollar market has a simple structure in the week to 16 October 2026. Two commodity sectors, mining and agriculture, sold banks far more dollars than they bought back. A different group of sectors, led by oil and energy and manufacturing, did the opposite. Trade, the biggest sector by turnover, swung from heavy net buying to small net selling. The result is a banking system that bought USD 121.35 million more than it sold, a shilling that moved only 0.07% to TZS 2,633.12 per dollar, and a question: how dependent is that stability on two sectors?","The shilling is being held steady by dollars from two commodity sectors that are spent largely by one import-heavy sector. That works as long as the flow continues, and the report gives no sign it will stop. But the cushion varied by a factor of nearly two in a single week, and the same dollar needs push on prices and costs every working day. The next four tabs show who supplies, what oil needs, how it feeds through to the economy, and where the stress points are.",yes,no,1,9
