Tanzania,Digital Economy,"Africa Cybercrime Raid Nets 1,462 Arrests, $105m",https://uchumi360.com/u/t/operation-serengeti-iii-arrests,Uchumi360,22 Oct 2026,,no,"Operation Serengeti III led to 1,462 arrests across 21 countries, disrupted 10,909 malicious networks and recovered more than US$105 million, while investigators linked the targeted cybercrime operations to losses exceeding US$385 million. The cases reveal an increasingly industrialised cybercrime economy spanning mobile money, investment scams, BEC, cryptocurrency and malicious digital lending.","African law enforcement agencies have arrested 1,462 suspects, disrupted 10,909 malicious cyber networks and recovered more than US$105 million in illicit funds in one of the continent’s largest coordinated cybercrime operations, but the financial evidence behind Operation Serengeti III suggests that the significance of the crackdown extends well beyond the number of arrests. INTERPOL says investigators linked the criminal activity uncovered during the operation to more than US$385 million in financial losses and identified 65,929 victims, while cases ranged from investment fraud and business email compromise to malicious lending applications, cryptocurrency enabled laundering and transnational scam centres. The operation ran from June 29 to August 31, 2026 and involved authorities from 21 countries, including Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, Rwanda, Nigeria, South Africa, Ghana, Zambia and the United States.","The numbers provide another indication that cybercrime in Africa is becoming less like a collection of isolated internet scams and more like an organised economic activity operating across payments systems, telecommunications networks, cryptocurrency infrastructure and national borders. INTERPOL’s African Cyberthreat Assessment 2026 estimates that reported cybercrime-related losses across Africa have more than doubled from about US$192 million in 2024 to US$484 million, while artificial intelligence is now involved in an estimated 55 percent of reported cybercrime on the continent. The same assessment identifies East Africa as a significant centre for mobile money fraud and ransomware targeting infrastructure, making Operation Serengeti III particularly relevant to economies where digital payments have expanded much faster than the investigative systems required to trace money across banks, telecom operators, cryptocurrency platforms and jurisdictions.",yes,no,1,9
