Tanzania,Governance,Jailed Tanzania opposition leader vows hunger strike,https://www.modernghana.com/news/1539056/jailed-tanzania-opposition-leader-vows-hunger-stri.html,Modern Ghana,25 Oct 2026,,no,"Detained Tanzanian opposition leader Tundu Lissu plans to go on a hunger strike on Monday to demand prison authorities allow him to privately meet his lawyers, his party said.","Lissu, a longtime critic of the Chama Cha Mapinduzi party ruling Tanzania since independence in 1961, has been jailed while waiting for the outcome of a trial for treason -- a charge that carries a potential death sentence.","Having survived an assassination attempt in 2017, Lissu was arrested in April 2025 on accusations he incited the public to boycott that year's elections.",yes,no,4,6
Tanzania,Agrifood & Climate,BL Agro Plans $739m Tanzania Agriculture Investment,https://uchumi360.com/c/infrastructure-industrialisation/india-agro-plans-739,Uchumi360,23 Oct 2026,,no,"India’s BL Agro plans to invest about US$739 million in Tanzania over ten years, beginning with a large sunflower oil complex before expanding into dairy genetics, agriculture and manufacturing. The proposal comes as Tanzania tries to close an edible oil deficit exceeding 400,000 tonnes and cut an annual import bill of more than US$200 million.","Indian food manufacturer BL Agro Industries plans to invest ₹70 billion, approximately US$739 million at current exchange rates, in Tanzania over the next decade, beginning with a large vegetable oil complex and later expanding into dairy genetics, livestock, agricultural production, farm equipment and food manufacturing. The proposed investment would give the Bareilly based company its first major production base outside India and comes as Tanzania is attempting to close one of the largest structural gaps in its agricultural economy: a domestic edible oil market that consumes more than twice what local producers currently supply.","BL Agro announced the Tanzania expansion on October 22 after a company delegation held discussions with Tanzanian officials and other stakeholders. Managing Director Ashish Khandelwal said the programme would combine Tanzanian agricultural resources with Indian processing and production technology, while Tanzania’s High Commissioner to India, Anisa Mbega, welcomed the proposal as an opportunity to increase local processing, employment, technology transfer and farmer incomes. The investment is expected to be phased over ten years rather than deployed immediately, making the US$739 million figure a planned capital programme rather than money already invested in the Tanzanian economy.",yes,no,0,10
