Africa,Macroeconomy,South Africa has lowered its growth forecast for 2026 to 1.2% and temporarily cut fuel taxes.,https://news.chemnet.com/news-11086.html,ChemNet News,26 Oct 2026,,no,"On October 21, South Africa's Finance Minister Enoch Godongwana submitted the Medium-Term Budget Policy Statement to Parliament in Cape Town, the country's legislative capital, announcing a downward revision of the nation's economic growth forecast for 2026 and the implementation of a phased temporary fuel tax relief policy to offset inflation and operational pressures caused by rising international oil prices.","Growth forecast downgraded, geopolitical and climate risks pose external disturbances","This medium-term budget cut South Africa's economic growth forecast for 2026 from the 1.6% estimated in February to 1.2% . Looking ahead, South African officials expect the economic growth rate to rebound to 1.6% in 2027 and reach around 2% by 2029.",yes,no,1,9
