Burundi,Jobs & Employment,Burundi Cut Inflation From 45% To 8.6%. The Cost Was Much Tighter Money,https://uchumi360.com/r/b/burundi-cut-inflation-cost,Uchumi360,22 Oct 2026,,no,"Burundi cut inflation from about 45% in April 2025 to 8.6% a year later after restricting central bank financing of government and tightening monetary conditions. The adjustment restored greater price stability, but weaker credit, scarce foreign exchange and a large exchange rate gap show how much work remains.","Burundi entered 2025 with inflation running at levels that made ordinary economic planning increasingly difficult. Prices were rising rapidly, foreign exchange remained scarce, the gap between the official and parallel exchange rates was large, and the government had been relying heavily on domestic financing, including advances from the central bank. By April 2025, annual inflation had reached about 45%, placing Burundi among the most inflationary economies in Africa. Twelve months later, inflation was 8.6%, its lowest level since June 2021, after a policy reversal that restricted government access to central bank financing, reduced liquidity in the banking system and forced considerably greater discipline on both fiscal and monetary policy.","The scale of that decline is unusual. Burundi did benefit from lower food prices and more favourable import prices, while stronger coffee and gold exports brought additional foreign exchange into an economy that badly needed it. But the fall in inflation was not simply the result of external conditions improving. The government and the Banque de la République du Burundi changed the way the economy was being financed. Central bank advances to the government were frozen from the middle of 2025, fiscal policy became tighter and monetary conditions were deliberately restricted. Broad money growth, which had reached 29.3% in 2024, slowed to 19.4% in 2025 as excess liquidity declined. In January 2026, the authorities consolidated this change through a Macroeconomic Stabilization Plan covering fiscal consolidation, monetary tightening, foreign exchange reform and structural measures intended to address some of the imbalances that had accumulated during the preceding years.",yes,no,4,6
