Lesotho,Macroeconomy,"Lesotho: Unions call for sustained public investment, stronger social alliances, and regional solidarity",https://www.ei-ie.org/en/item/33187:lesotho-unions-call-for-sustained-public-investment-stronger-social-alliances-and-regional-solidarity,Education International,23 Oct 2026,,no,"Education unions in Lesotho are warning that growing poverty, widening inequality, and years of insufficient investment are putting the right to education at risk for thousands of children and young people. While access to education has expanded over recent decades, many families are struggling to cover the costs associated with schooling, forcing some learners out of the education system before they can complete their studies.","The Lesotho Teachers' Trade Union (LTTU), an affiliate of Education International (EI), says quality public education cannot be built on access alone. It requires sustained public funding, equitable investment, and a collective commitment to education as a public good rather than a private burden. Drawing on its work through the Go Public! Fund Education campaign, the union has highlighted both the challenges facing Lesotho's education system and the opportunities to build broader public support for education funding.","According to the union, many of the pressures confronting schools reflect broader social and economic challenges. Despite education continuing to receive a significant share of the national budget, rising costs, inflation, and funding gaps in areas such as infrastructure, inclusive education, digital learning and learner support services are undermining progress. Teachers' unions argue that if education is truly regarded as the foundation of democracy, social justice and national development, it must be treated as a national priority in public budgets.",yes,no,1,9
