Sudan,Agrifood & Climate,Today in Africa: Ethiopians flee to Kenya as Sudan drought worsens famine risk,https://www.okayafrica.com/today-in-africa-october-23-2026-hundreds-of-ethiopians-flee-into-kenya-el-nino-drought-threatens-new-famine-in-sudan/1441088,OkayAfrica,23 Oct 2026,,no,"Every day, OkayAfrica shares a roundup of news we’re following but haven’t published as full articles. These short updates cover what’s happening across the continent — in culture, politics, and beyond. For more stories like these, check out our News page for updates from across the regions.","Hundreds of Ethiopians, mostly women, children, and young people, crossed into Kenya through several entry points in Moyale after fighting broke out in the Ethiopian border town of Moyale on Wednesday, Kenyan police and the Kenya Red Cross said. Kenyan police say the arrivals are fleeing tensions between leaders of Ethiopia's Oromia and Somali regional states. Gunfire was heard on the Ethiopian side from about 5:50 a.m. on Thursday and continued afterward. Stray bullets reached the Kenyan side, where a woman was shot in the neck, and a police officer was struck on the head when a bullet hit a metal bar supporting a tent at the Moyale police station's football ground. Police did not identify the source of the bullets, and Kenya's Moyale town remained calm, with police on high alert.","El Niño has brought damaging drought to Sudan just as a lull in fighting had raised hopes that a good harvest would ease hunger. Rainfall in the July to September peak season was the lowest in more than three decades nationwide, according to U.S. Geological Survey data, and in North Darfur it was the lowest since 1984, the year a famine devastated Sudan and Ethiopia. The United Nations-backed hunger monitor, the IPC, warned in an October 12 alert that the risk of famine is growing in many conflict-affected areas, with drought compounding dire conditions. The IPC said in June that 5.1 million people faced severe hunger, and it is updating the numbers, which had been expected to fall once harvesting began in November and December. The World Food Programme (WFP) forecasts 350,000 more people will face severe hunger by the end of 2026 and 1.2 million more by August 2027, with Darfur and Kordofan hit hardest. Sudan's government has played down the threat, with Agriculture Minister Esmat Qureshi saying this year's harvest would be lower but ""not significantly less than last year's.""",yes,no,2,8
