South Africa,Macroeconomy,South Africa’s Political Risk Is Greater Than the Bond Market Thinks,https://www.thecommonsense.co.za/Editorials/south-africa-s-political-risk-greater-than-bond-market,The Common Sense,26 Oct 2026,,no,"The yield differential between South Africa’s 10-year government bond and the equivalent United States (US) Treasury bond has narrowed to levels last seen between 2004 and 2007. The narrowing reflects a marked improvement in the market’s assessment of South African assets, particularly since the formation of the Government of National Unity (GNU) following the May 2024 election. There are, however, compelling reasons to question whether that improved assessment is justified by the underlying political and economic circumstances of the country.","The differential is a useful broad indicator of the additional yield investors receive for holding South African rather than American government debt. The US Treasury market remains the global benchmark for relatively risk-free dollar borrowing, while South African bonds must compensate investors for a range of additional risks. Because the two bonds are denominated in different currencies, the differential also reflects relative inflation, interest rate, and exchange rate expectations. Nevertheless, its compression is an important signal of greater investor confidence in South Africa.",The charts below sets out the differential since 1994 together with some of the risk events that caused it to spike.,yes,no,3,7
