Morocco,Agrifood & Climate,"""In Morocco, coastal areas with moderate temperatures are promising for pitaya trials""",https://www.freshplaza.com/north-america/article/9885908/in-morocco-coastal-areas-with-moderate-temperatures-are-promising-for-pitaya-trials/,FreshPlaza,23 Oct 2026,,no,"Water availability, shifting temperatures, and the search for new commercial opportunities are pushing Moroccan growers to rethink their crops. ""Moroccan agriculture is in the middle of a transformation. Among the species gaining attention is dragon fruit, or pitaya,"" says Ferdaous El Barnossi of Smidcom, a Moroccan greenhouse company. She explains why pitaya is drawing interest from growers and how a protected structure should be designed around it.","A climbing cactus native to tropical America, pitaya differs from classic fruit crops. Its CAM photosynthesis is a notable advantage. ""Unlike most cultivated plants, pitaya absorbs carbon dioxide mainly at night, which allows it to limit certain water losses,"" El Barnossi explains. ""This adaptation is an advantage in a context of pressure on water resources."" She adds that this does not mean it can grow without irrigation, as water remains important during growth, flowering and fruit sizing.","The published results she cites on yields are encouraging. ""A greenhouse study in Turkey recorded average yields of about 4.4 to 7.2 tonnes per hectare, depending on irrigation, and its authors point to potentials of 20 to 30 tonnes for plantations at full capacity. In southern Spain, a trial with the Royal Red variety in intensive hydroponics under plastic reached close to 40 tonnes per hectare, with 10,000 plants per hectare and manual pollination. This result is interesting, but it corresponds to a very particular intensive system."" For a first economic study, she suggests modeling 10, 20 and 30 tonnes per hectare at established production.",yes,no,2,8
