Ethiopia,Governance,African Civil Society Leaders Advocate for Peace,https://www.fcnl.org/updates/2026-10/african-civil-society-leaders-advocate-peace,Friends Committee On National Legislation,20 Oct 2026,,no,"In October 2026, FCNL partnered with the American Friends Service Committee (AFSC) and the Salama Hub to support a delegation of East African peace activists visiting Washington, D.C. to advocate to Congress and the State Department. Members of the delegation included religious leaders, humanitarian workers, grassroots organizers, and human rights defenders from Kenya, Ethiopia, Sudan, South Sudan, Somalia, and Uganda.","Peacebuilding efforts are most durable when they are guided and led by local peacebuilders based in affected communities. In the East and Horn of Africa, violent conflict has been a persistent challenge – but the work of local peacebuilders has been equally persistent.","Sudan has experienced three years of brutal warfare that has included crimes against humanity and genocide . Salwa, a Sudanese delegation member, has spent more than a decade advancing the cause of women, peace, and security, with a focus on protecting civilians, preventing violence against women, providing psychosocial support, and ensuring women’s meaningful participation in peace and decision-making. In her meetings on the Hill, Salwa advocated for the U.S. to prioritize funding and protection for Sudanese local responders, increase funding to support survivors of sexual and gender-based violence, and ensure unrestricted access to humanitarian aid.",yes,no,3,7
