Equatorial Guinea,Governance,“Dying in Detention”: Al Jazeera Investigates Immigrant Deaths in California’s Adelanto ICE Jail,https://www.democracynow.org/2026/10/23/dying_in_detention,Democracy Now!,23 Oct 2026,,no,"AMY GOODMAN : This is Democracy Now! , democracynow.org. I’m Amy Goodman in Los Angeles. Juan González is in Chicago.","We’re turning now to the rising numbers of deaths in ICE custody. At least 59 people have died in immigration detention since the start of Trump’s second term, the highest in more than a decade, and a growing number of people are dying from medical neglect. Emiliano Andrade-Covarrubias, a 56-year-old legal permanent resident who died earlier this month at an ICE detention center run by the private GEO Group in Laredo, Texas, has complained to his daughter for months about the lack of medical care in detention. At the Adelanto Processing Center, just north of where I am right now in Los Angeles, four out of five recent deaths were linked to substandard medical care. That’s according to the California attorney general’s report on ICE detention centers in California this year.","Journalist Natasha Del Toro and Al Jazeera’s Fault Lines investigated the deaths at the Adelanto Processing Center, which is run, yes, again, by the private GEO Group. This is a clip from the Fault Lines episode Dying in Detention .",yes,no,1,9
Equatorial Guinea,Governance,MKO: The Story of An Unlikely Hero of Democracy,https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/18/mko-the-story-of-an-unlikely-hero-of-democracy/,THISDAYLIVE,26 Oct 2026,,no,"Confession: I despised Gen Sani Abacha with the same intensity Obidients hate President Bola Tinubu. I thought I had overcome the ill-feeling after 28 years. Alas, I watched ‘MKO’ — the documentary on Bashorun Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola currently showing in film houses across Nigeria — and my emotions went haywire again. It brought back painful recollections of military rule and sickening memories of Abacha, the military head of state who chastised Nigeria with scorpions. Media repression and assassinations were the hallmarks of the Abacha regime. It was horror all over again. My emotions swung from nostalgia to despair, from anger to resentment.","Three things hit me instantly from the documentary. One, the incredible transformation of Abiola from an establishment person to, as it were, an activist for democracy — like a prince becoming a rebel. Two, the key role Abacha played in the June 12 annulment crisis and how he became the ultimate beneficiary. Three, the place of America in the logjam and the unending suspicion that it played a role in sustaining the annulment and even in the death of Abiola. I went away with a few lessons, chief of which is that life is not black and white. Hardline positions might seem principled and desirable, but pragmatism as an option should always be on the table in playing national politics.","Abiola was a friend of power. I remember when he declared his presidential bid ahead of the 1993 election, one well-respected activist said: “President Babangida wants to hand over to one of his friends.” The impression was that he would never have come out without assurances from the powers that be. And, indeed, he did seek and get assurances, according to the man himself. He had been a government insider from the 1970s, when he was in the telecoms equipment business and got huge public contracts. He subsequently went into politics in 1979 and was believed to have supported at least one military coup. As a friend of power, he was quite circumspect in his ways.",yes,no,2,8
